    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d311">

                <pb facs="0472" n="360" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg360"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7398</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">of a Conversation
                        With the Japanese Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Ariyoshi</hi>)<anchor n="96" xml:id="d311-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-10-03">October 3, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In the course of conversation with Mr. Ariyoshi he remarked that conditions in
                    North China were tending to settle down. There were one or two small matters
                    left to be settled, one relating to air transportation between “Manchukuo” and
                    China, which was part of the Tangku Truce. He casually stated that it would all
                    be so much simpler if China would recognize “Manchukuo”, as this would help to
                    settle many of the border questions. Japan, he said, was not interested in
                    starting a separatist government in North China, although there were a great
                    many Chinese officials who were out of jobs and were prepared to intrigue with
                    the Japanese military.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson Trusler
                    Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d311-f1" n="96" type="source" xml:id="d311-f1-content">
                <p>Copy transmitted to the Department by the Ambassador in China, in his despatch
                    No. 54, October 25; received November 18.</p>
            </note>
